#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""Admit and revoke host access for people and projects.

Root's 0600 people/<user>.conf records ordered member= lines, locked=true|false,
ssh=<card key> and expires=<card expiry or empty>. No device credential is admitted.
The follow reconciles the persons' project roles, locks, forge teams and
allowed signers from the organization's state it took.
Project locks leave other memberships, sessions and tokens intact. Current-key
revocation and last-membership removal revoke the whole account.

org follow, org refresh and org renew-pin run for root alone. Setup writes the organization
settings, forge host keys and initial admin pin, and makes its read-only deploy
key. Follow fetches through an isolated network-capable unit, reconciles project
memberships and publishes project files with read ACLs for unlocked members.
Leaving every project records the lock day; follow purges the account seven days
later. A person still listed in a project is locked at most, never purged.
Nothing is followed until refresh, given the host admin's central token once on
stdin, has had Porelo add the read key; the token is kept nowhere. A forge project waits
for refresh to receive a token for migration. Empty stdin follows without migrating.
Refresh answers status after following immediately.
Each person is checked alone, naming bad ones while the rest applies; every revoked
login stays revoked with its last applied entry, and the answer goes to root's 0600
organization/applied.json.

With account= in forge.conf, admission creates the login and the porelo-<user>
gate and no token, since Forgejo keeps only a token's hash; lock also revokes
each <agent>-<user> a 6j host's agents= names. forge-token, by sudo alone, deletes
every token of the caller's login and answers one new token alone on stdout;
that token is written to no file. Lock prohibits all those logins and deletes all their tokens
through the forge's private socket API; its temporary admin token stays
in memory. The follow applies card keys and locks every person whose current key it revokes.

task <task>, by sudo alone, reads one Ed25519 user certificate of at most 16 KiB
on stdin from a private temporary file with ssh-keygen -L, which checks its
signature. Its signing key must be the caller's key in organization/applied.json, not
revoked; exactly one principal is task:<project>:<task> (or task:default:<task>) of a project
the caller is a member of; it must hold now and end. Before any record, Porelo's own
task check-certificate judges it against that key and the KRL, in a unit with no home and no
network, and must answer for the same certificate. Then root's
0600 /var/lib/porelo-host/tasks/<user>/<task>.json records it, never replaced,
and the gate's token porelo-task-<task> of porelo-<user> is made anew and answered
alone on stdout. task --end <task> deletes that token; task --show <fingerprint>
answers the record of that task key, read-only, to root or any unlocked person;
sweep, root's alone from its daily timer, deletes every task token whose recorded
certificate has ended. Task and forge-token calls delete their temporary admin
token before reporting success.

verify-push, run by the socket unit Forgejo's pre-receive hook connects to,
answers only the forge account's peer. It maps the hook's repository and
quarantine into the forge volume, has Porelo's commit verify judge the new
commits in a unit as the forge account with no network and no home but that
repository read-only, against the project's keys in force, their certifiers
of the project's own tasks, the KRL and the project's task certificates valid
now, and answers the hook
200 (each accepted commit appended to root's 0600
/var/lib/porelo-host/arrivals/<project>/<repository>.jsonl), 403 with Porelo's
lines, or 500. The follow arms each repository of the project's organization and, for
one whose record has no armed line yet, records its history so far as
migrated, not judged here.
arrivals <project> <repository> answers that record to root or a person of the
project.

Matrix admission stores no token. The gate authenticates the socket's peer.
Purge runs the transport's expel <login> as porelo-matrix, the gate's admin token
on its stdin alone; a refusal stops removal. A lock leaves the Matrix account: the
gate refuses a login out of porelo-people, and the person may come back.

Validation precedes changes; publication of the record is last. Command failures
can leave partial system changes and are reported without their captured output.
Do not manually admit a partial account: inspect and remove its partial access
before retrying. Calls serialize on root's existing forge.conf, without creating
a lock file during validation. Purge removes the record; another purge then
refuses the unknown person with 65, leaving the same state.

The only test hook, _test_entry, exists only when imported under another name.
An installed call runs as __main__ with -I and accepts no path, identity, runner,
or hook overrides from arguments, stdin or the environment.
"""

